{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":1815,"output":2147,"cacheRead":125696,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1462,"totalTokens":129658,"cost":{"input":0.0007845337500000001,"output":0.00390754,"cacheRead":0.009150668800000001,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.013842742550000002}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"At each 1s real =10 sim min, same agents waiting. This is definitely starvation or excessive wait. Notice agents at floor0 wanting up (id24 etc) wait from 12:52 to 1:20 (28 sim min) while car keeps opening at floor0? In sample at 1:20, id24 still floor0 to4. The elevator at 1:20 is floor1 direction up. It must have opened at0 recently. Why didn't id24 board? Because when car opens at0, capacity 4, only 4 of ~15 board, others remain. But over 28 sim min there should be multiple trips. Yet id24 maybe not first in line due random? All WAIT_AT_PANEL process in agent order; agents with lower id always board first (id19-23 before id24). So id24 waits until all lower-id waiting at floor0 board. That's queue but should clear after 3 trips. 28 sim min at 600x is 2.8s real; elevator cycles maybe one stop per ~1s real? Wait at 600x, each frame sim advances ~9.6 min? No: 600x means 600 sim seconds per real second = 10 sim minutes per real second. 1s real = 10 sim min. The elevator state cycle per frame ~0.016 real sec = 0.16 sim min. So in 28 sim min = 2.8 real sec = ~170 frames. That's many cycles. Should clear.\n\nMaybe agents at floor0 with WAIT_AT_PANEL are not actually at the elevator but at cafe etc? Their action floor0. Hmm.\n\nKey observation: elevator at floor2 DOOR_OPEN direction DOWN with upCalls [0,1] and down calls [5,4,3,2,1], dest[0], passengers4. At floor2 direction down, opens. Waiting at floor2 to0 (id23) should be accepted: isAcceptingAt(2,-1) true, capacity free? passengers4 full -> currentCapacityFree = 4 - (4+0) =0, so not accepted! The car is full of riders going to0. It opens at floor2 to maybe serve down call but full, no one can board. It then closes and continues to0. This repeats. But when car is full, it should not open for hall calls unless passengers need that floor. It's opening at floor2 because down call2, but full. It then wastes a stop. However after reaching 0, riders exit, car goes up, and floor2 down call remains. On the way up, it might serve up calls first, then eventually down. But if it keeps filling with up riders at 0 to upper floors, it may return down full and again skip picking up down callers. This is classic full-car starvation: at floor0, there are many up callers; car takes 4 to upper floors; on the way down it may be full of down callers from upper floors, passes floor2 full; floor2 waiters never board. Eventually after up demand drops, they should. But lunch rush continuous.\n\nSpec says \"No full-car lobby starvation... repeated calls from current floor remain queued for next trip but must not cause DOOR_CLOSING -> DOOR_OPENING at same floor.\" Not this. But our simulation capacity 4 and occupancy 60 with 20 workers + visitors creates heavy demand. Yet expected \"mild queuing\". 30/60 waiting for 30+ sim min is not mild; maybe because at 600x the clock outpaces elevator due state machine one transition per frame. Wait, at high timeScale, motionDt=~10 sim sec per frame. The elevator car can only process one state per frame, and door open/close each take one frame regardless of sim time. Real-time throughput in frames: each stop = 4 frames. At 60fps, 15 stops/sec real. But sim clock advances 10 sim min/sec real. Each stop serves 4 people, 60 people/sec real. Over 2.8s, 168 people. Should be fine. Unless the browser frame rate is much lower due 60 agents and O(n^2)? Our probe headless maybe ~60fps. Runtime frames 92 over 5.5s ~17fps? Wait runtime frames=92 over ~5.5s =16.7 fps. With rendering 2667 objects and many transparent, frame rate maybe ~15-20fps. At 20fps, stops/sec =5, capacity4 =>20 people/sec real; 2.8s =>56. Still enough. But maybe frame rate drops to 5fps with 60 agents? 1.25 stops/sec *4=5 people/sec; 2.8s=14. Not enough for 30. Hmm.\n\nBut this is at 600x extreme; default 120x is 5x slower sim clock, and frame rate similar, so elevator has 5x more real time per sim minute. Waiting should be much less. Runtime check boosts to 600 though, but only checks motion. The prompt says expected morning queue at 120x. At 600x maybe overloaded. The user might view default 120x. Still, we should improve throughput to reduce waiting at high speed and generally.\n\nMain issues:\n1. Car opens for hall calls when full, wasting time. Could avoid opening at a floor if no passenger destination and no capacity for that hall call? But hall call direction should be served eventually. If full, skip hall call? We can have logic not stop for hall calls if capacity free <=0, except maybe if it's a destination. But the car may be full with riders going past; skipping hall calls is realistic. We can modify `_pickNextTarget` when passengers.size>=maxCapacity: only choose destinations, not hall calls. That prevents full car from stopping at hall calls. It will serve riders first, then return for calls. This improves.\n\n2. At high timeScale, boarding/disembarking handshake may not complete in same frame? It did. But state WAITING means agents waiting not boarded due capacity.\n\n3. Number of visitors doing office-floor activities is high: 23% meetings + 15% lounge =38% of visitors go upstairs. At occupancy 60, ~20 visitors, 8 upstairs per cycle, plus 20 workers, demand high. That's per spec though.\n\n4. Worker lunch creates surge.\n\nGiven default 120x, maybe okay. But we can make elevator faster / capacity still 4 hard limit. Cannot exceed. Could reduce door cycle time? Already min 3 sim sec; at 120x, 3 sim sec =0.025 real sec ~2 frames. Fine.\n\nLet's test at default 120x for a longer real time to see waiting counts. At 120x, to reach noon takes (690-450)*60/120=120s real. Too long for probe but can wait 120s. Maybe use 300x (half) 60s. The earlier 300x probe at 8:36 had 18 waiting. At default 120x, 8:36 is 33s real; waiting maybe. Let's test 120x for 30s -> 8:30, and maybe 60s ->9:30. This is more representative."}}
