 reaching 0, riders exit, car goes up, and floor2 down call remains. On the way up, it might serve up calls first, then eventually down. But if it keeps filling with up riders at 0 to upper floors, it may return down full and again skip picking up down callers. This is classic full-car starvation: at floor0, there are many up callers; car takes 4 to upper floors; on the way down it may be full of down callers from upper floors, passes floor2 full; floor2 waiters never board. Eventually after up demand drops, they should. But lunch rush continuous.\n\nSpec says \"No full-car lobby starvation... repeated calls from current floor remain queued for next trip but must not cause DOOR_CLOSING -> DOOR_OPENING at same floor.\" Not this. But our simulation capacity 4 and occupancy 60 with 20 workers + visitors creates heavy demand. Yet expected \"mild queuing\". 30/60 waiting for 30+ sim min is not mild; maybe because at 600x the clock outpaces elevator due state machine one transition per frame. Wait, at high timeScale, motionDt=~10 sim sec per frame. The elevator car can only process one state per frame, and door open/close each take one frame regardless of sim time. Real-time throughput in frames: each stop = 4 frames. At 60fps, 15 stops/sec real. But sim clock advances 10 sim min/sec real. Each stop serves 4 people, 60 people/sec real. Over 2.8s, 168 people. Should be fine. Unless the browser frame rate is much lower due 60 agents and O(n^2)? Our probe headless maybe ~60fps. Runtime frames 92 over 5.5s ~17fps? Wait runtime frames=92 over ~5.5s =16.7 fps. With rendering 2667 objects and many transparent, frame rate maybe ~15-20fps. At 20fps, stops/sec =5, capacity4 =>20 people/sec real; 2.8s =>56. Still enough. But maybe frame rate drops to 5fps with 60 agents? 1.25 stops/sec *4=5 people/sec; 2.8s=14. Not enough for 30. Hmm.\n\nBut this is at 600x extreme; default 120x is 5x slower sim clock, and frame rate similar, so elevator has 5x more real time per sim minute. Waiting should be much less. Runtime check boosts to 600 though, but only checks motion. The prompt says expected morning queue at 120x. At 600x maybe overloaded. The user might view default 120x. Still, we should improve throughput to reduce waiting at high speed and generally.\n\nMain issues:\n1. Car opens for hall calls when full, wasting time. Could avoid opening at a floor if no passenger destination and no capacity for that hall call? But hall call direction should be served eventually. If full, skip hall call? We can have logic not stop for hall calls if capacity free <=0, except maybe if it's a destination. But the car may be full with riders going past; skipping hall calls is realistic. We can modify `_pickNextTarget` when passengers.size>=maxCapacity: only choose destinations, not hall calls. That prevents full car from stopping at hall calls. It will serve riders first, then return for calls. This improves.\n\n2. At high timeScale, boarding/disembarking handshake may not complete in same frame? It did. But state WAITING means agents waiting not boarded due capacity.\n\n3. Number of visitors doing office-floor activities is high: 23% meetings + 15% lounge =38% of visitors go upstairs. At occupancy 60, ~20 visitors, 8 upstairs per cycle, plus 20 workers, demand high. That's per spec though.\n\n4. Worker lunch creates surge.\n\nGiven default 120x, maybe okay. But we can make elevator faster / capacity still 4 hard limit. Cannot exceed. Could reduce door cycle time? Already min 3 sim sec; at 120x, 3 sim sec =0.025 real sec ~2 frames. Fine.\n\nLet's test at default 120x for a longer real time to see waiting counts. At 120x, to reach noon takes (690-450)*60/120=120s real. Too long for probe but can wait 120s. Maybe use 300x (half) 60s. The earlier 300x probe at 8:36 had 18 waiting. At default 120x, 8:36 is 33s real; waiting maybe. Let's test 120x for 30s -> 8:30, and maybe 60s ->9:30. This is more representative.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"t