{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"At default 120x, no waiting and healthy. Good. At 600x overloaded due frame rate/time scale. The spec says timeScale pure multiplier and elevator should scale. It expects high speed maybe. But runtime only 5s. User might use slider. Could improve high-speed throughput by making elevator process multiple stops per tick? The lockstep model says motionDt=realDt*timeScale and elevator tick(dt) should move accordingly. Our logic handles large dt but only one state transition per tick, so at high dt it loses throughput. We could modify logic tick to process sub-steps or continue transitions within one tick, so at 600x elevator can complete multiple stops per frame. That would make high speed behavior better and satisfy \"lockstep\". The spec says tick(dt) one-frame advance; but can process multiple state transitions. Let's implement internal loop with remaining time, max e.g. 64 iterations, handling state machine with small substeps? Simpler: in tick(dt), loop while remaining>0 and transitions possible, consuming time. But door OPEN requires waiting min time; with dt=10, it could open, hold min, close, move to next floor, etc., all in one tick, potentially skipping waiting agents because agents only board between frames! That's bad: car would leave before agents can board. In real lockstep, agents also should update within substeps, but our agent dispatch happens after elevator.tick once per frame. So one state per tick ensures agents can interact. At high speed, this bottleneck is inherent to frame-based handshake. We can mitigate by making elevator wait for pending? Waiting agents don't reserve until door open. If elevator processes multiple states in one tick, it might close before agents board. Could add a minimum door open frames? Hard.\n\nAlternative: at high timeScale, clamp effective elevator dt to a maximum (e.g. 3 sim sec per frame) while agents move at full? That decouples motion vs clock, which spec forbids? It says don't clamp motion below timeScale. But we can choose elevator travel speed physically high enough? The bottleneck is state transitions per frame, not speed. We can make door open state persist at least one extra frame by requiring e.g. `doorTimer >= minDoorOpenS` AND `_openTicks >= 1`? At dt=10, min door 3; open tick sets doorTimer0; next frame tick adds10 and closes if pending empty. Waiting agents had one frame to board (the frame it opened). They did board up to 4. That's fine. But full car opens at hall call floor and waits one frame, no one boards, wastes a frame. Removing full-car hall stops helps.\n\nThe bigger high-speed issue may simply be insufficient capacity relative to demand because each frame at 600x advances 10 sim minutes; a full lunch rush has 60 people wanting elevator in a short sim window. The elevator needs to do 15 trips *4 frames =60 frames =1s real at 60fps. If headless fps ~10, 6s real, during which sim advances 60 min. So waiting. At default 120x, same 60 frames =6s real but sim advances 12 min, so it keeps up. The timeScale lockstep model inherently stresses frame rate. The spec explicitly says don't decouple; so high speed queue may be expected. Fine.\n\nStill, we can implement full-car skip hall calls to improve. Let's do that: in `_pickNextTarget`, if passengers.size >= maxCapacity, pool only destinations. But be careful tests: Test 1 car full, dests above, leftover up0 -> only dests, passes. Test 2 full? one passenger, not full. Test 3 one. Good. Test 4 no passengers. So add before passenger branch:\n```\nconst full = this.passengers.size >= this.maxCapacity;\nif (full && dests.length) {\n  // choose nearest dest in direction/any\n  ...\n}\n```\nBut current passenger branch already includes hall calls ahead. Modify condition to only include matching hall calls if capacity free >0. `const canTakeHall = this.currentCapacityFree() > 0;` Note pendingBoarders count maybe. If full, ahead = dests ahead. Good.\n\nAlso `_reconsiderWhileMoving` should skip hall calls if full. Add condition.\n\nThis prevents wasted stops. Do it.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"At default 120x, no waiting and healthy. Good. At 600x overloaded due frame rate/time scale. The spec says timeScale pure multiplier and elevator should scale. It expects high speed maybe. But runtime only 5s. User might use slider. Could improve high-speed throughput by making elevator process multiple stops per tick? The lockstep model says motionDt=realDt*timeScale and elevator tick(dt) should move accordingly. Our logic handles large dt but only one state transition per tick, so at high dt it loses throughput. We could modify logic tick to process sub-steps or continue transitions within one tick, so at 600x elevator can complete multiple stops per frame. That would make high speed behavior better and satisfy \\\"lockstep\\\". The spec says tick(dt) one-frame advance; but can process multiple state transitions. Let's implement internal loop with remaining time, max e.g. 64 iterations, handling state machine with small substeps? Simpler: in tick(dt), loop while remaining>0 and transitions possible, consuming time. But door OPEN requires waiting min time; with dt=10, it could open, hold min, close, move to next floor, etc., all in one tick, potentially skipping waiting agents because agents only board between frames! That's bad: car would leave before agents can board. In real lockstep, agents also should update within substeps, but our agent dispatch happens after elevator.tick once per frame. So one state per tick ensures agents can interact. At high speed, this bottleneck is inherent to frame-based handshake. We can mitigate by making elevator wait for pending? Waiting agents don't reserve until door open. If elevator processes multiple states in one tick, it might close before agents board. Could add a minimum door open frames? Hard.\\n\\nAlternative: at high timeScale, clamp effective elevator dt to a maximum (e.g. 3 sim sec per frame) while agents move at full? That decouples motion vs clock, which spec forbids? It says don't clamp motion below timeScale. But we can choose elevator travel speed physically high enough? The bottleneck is state transitions per frame, not speed. We can make door open state persist at least one extra frame by requiring e.g. `doorTimer >= minDoorOpenS` AND `_openTicks >= 1`? At dt=10, min door 3; open tick sets doorTimer0; next frame tick adds10 and closes if pending empty. Waiting agents had one frame to board (the frame it opened). They did board up to 4. That's fine. But full car opens at hall call floor and waits one frame, no one boards, wastes a frame. Removing full-car hall stops helps.\\n\\nThe bigger high-speed issue may simply be insufficient capacity relative to demand because each frame at 600x advances 10 sim minutes; a full lunch rush has 60 people wanting elevator in a short sim window. The elevator needs to do 15 trips *4 frames =60 frames =1s real at 60fps. If headless fps ~10, 6s real, during which sim advances 60 min. So waiting. At default 120x, same 60 frames =6s real but sim advances 12 min, so it keeps up. The timeScale lockstep model inherently stresses frame rate. The spec explicitly says don't decouple; so high speed queue may be expected. Fine.\\n\\nStill, we can implement full-car skip hall calls to improve. Let's do that: in `_pickNextTarget`, if passengers.size >= maxCapacity, pool only destinations. But be careful tests: Test 1 car full, dests above, leftover up0 -> only dests, passes. Test 2 full? one passenger, not full. Test 3 one. Good. Test 4 no passengers. So add before passenger branch:\\n```\\nconst full = this.passengers.size >= this.maxCapacity;\\nif (full && dests.length) {\\n  // choose nearest dest in direction/any\\n  ...\\n}\\n```\\nBut current passenger branch already includes hall calls ahead. Modify condition to only include matching hall calls if ca